WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
Welcome to Session one.

2
00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:05.000
This session is the vocabulary.

3
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:18.975
Everything in this course is built from about a dozen mathematical ideas, and today we go through all of them with real numbers, so that when they turn up later you recognise them rather than meeting them for the first time in the middle of something else.

4
00:00:18.975 --> 00:00:22.225
Two things about how to use this recording.

5
00:00:22.225 --> 00:00:25.150
First, most of this will be familiar.

6
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:27.275
That is intended.

7
00:00:27.275 --> 00:00:36.900
The aim is not to teach you mathematics you have never seen; it is to get everybody to the same place, and to connect each idea to where it comes back in the course.

8
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:43.125
Second, and more practically: this is the one session where listening is genuinely worse than reading.

9
00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:49.050
A formula on a slide is one glance; the same formula read aloud is a long sentence.

10
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:56.450
So I will tell you what each expression means and walk the arithmetic slowly, and leave the symbols on the screen for you to look at.

11
00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:02.175
If you are listening without the slides, this is the session to come back to with the page open.

12
00:01:02.175 --> 00:01:07.600
Three blocks: vectors and matrices, calculus, and probability and statistics.

13
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:13.675
Everything in this lecture is also written out in full on the Session one page of the course book.

14
00:01:13.675 --> 00:01:21.900
The page is the reference and this is the walk through it, so whenever a number or a name goes past too quickly, that is where to look it up.

15
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.925
I will say which part of the page we are in as each block starts.

16
00:01:26.525 --> 00:01:34.575
Here is everything we will cover, on one slide, and what I want you to notice is the right-hand column: where each idea comes back.

17
00:01:34.575 --> 00:01:40.525
The dot product becomes a profile score in Session five and a perceptron in Session seven.

18
00:01:40.525 --> 00:01:43.425
They are the same arithmetic, twice.

19
00:01:43.425 --> 00:01:48.250
The gradient trains every model in the course, from Session seven onwards.

20
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:53.600
The chain rule is the one idea that backpropagation is built from, in Session nine.

21
00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:06.725
Logarithms of probabilities give you log-odds scores, which are substitution matrices and profile scores in Sessions four and five, and cross-entropy, which is the loss function for every classifier from Session eight on.

22
00:02:06.725 --> 00:02:10.775
So this is not a list of mathematics you might one day need.

23
00:02:10.775 --> 00:02:18.125
It is a list of the things that are about to appear, with real numbers attached, and each one is here because it is used later.

24
00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:21.625
It is also not a complete mathematics course.

25
00:02:21.625 --> 00:02:23.775
Plenty is left out.

26
00:02:23.775 --> 00:02:26.700
What is here is what this course uses.

27
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.775
Block one.

28
00:02:28.775 --> 00:02:31.225
Vectors and matrices.

29
00:02:31.225 --> 00:02:35.500
On the page, this block is the section "Vectors and matrices".

30
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:44.250
The whole block builds towards one operation: the dot product, which turns out to be the single most important piece of arithmetic in this course.

31
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:49.750
Everything before it is preparation, and everything after it is consequences.

32
00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:53.325
A vector is an ordered list of numbers.

33
00:02:53.325 --> 00:02:55.875
That is the whole definition.

34
00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:57.850
Two operations.

35
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:04.000
You add two vectors by adding them entry by entry: first plus first, second plus second.

36
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.700
And you scale a vector by multiplying every entry by the same number.

37
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.100
The dimension is just how many entries it has.

38
00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:24.300
Now, the biological example that we will use all session: take a protein and compute the fraction of its residues that are alanine, the fraction that are cysteine, and so on through all twenty amino acids.

39
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.400
That is twenty numbers in a fixed order, so it is a twenty-dimensional vector.

40
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.500
Every protein becomes a point in twenty-dimensional space.

41
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:38.175
And then the geometric picture, which is what the rest of this block builds on.

42
00:03:38.175 --> 00:03:43.150
Think of a vector as an arrow from the origin: it has a direction and a length.

43
00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:46.500
For two or three dimensions you can draw that.

44
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:56.625
For twenty you cannot, and it does not matter: the arithmetic is identical, and the geometric words, direction, length, angle, keep working even where you cannot picture them.

45
00:03:56.625 --> 00:04:04.225
That is worth saying because people stall at exactly this point, feeling they ought to be able to visualise twenty dimensions.

46
00:04:04.225 --> 00:04:06.025
Nobody can.

47
00:04:06.025 --> 00:04:10.625
You reason with the algebra and borrow the words from two dimensions.

48
00:04:11.225 --> 00:04:14.825
The length of a vector, which is also called its norm.

49
00:04:14.825 --> 00:04:17.525
It is Pythagoras, extended.

50
00:04:17.525 --> 00:04:30.575
In two dimensions, the length of the arrow from the origin to the point three, four is the square root of three squared plus four squared, which is the square root of nine plus sixteen, which is the square root of twenty-five, which is five.

51
00:04:30.575 --> 00:04:33.575
The familiar three-four-five triangle.

52
00:04:33.575 --> 00:04:39.775
In n dimensions, exactly the same: square every entry, add them all up, take the square root.

53
00:04:39.775 --> 00:04:42.600
And notice what this generalises.

54
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:47.175
For a single number, the absolute value is its distance from zero.

55
00:04:47.175 --> 00:04:52.950
The norm is that same idea for a list of numbers: how far this point is from the origin.

56
00:04:52.950 --> 00:05:03.875
We will need it in two places in this block: to normalise a vector, which means to strip its length away and keep only its direction, and to understand what the dot product is measuring.

57
00:05:04.475 --> 00:05:07.475
A matrix is a rectangle of numbers.

58
00:05:07.475 --> 00:05:13.150
If it has n rows and m columns, we say its shape is n by m, rows first, always.

59
00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:20.425
In biology, a matrix is usually a data table: one row per sample or per protein, one column per feature.

60
00:05:20.425 --> 00:05:24.650
Gene expression: fifty samples by twenty thousand genes.

61
00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:29.850
Protein features: a thousand proteins by twenty amino-acid fractions.

62
00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:34.850
And a tensor is the same idea continued into three or more dimensions.

63
00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:44.475
A batch of thirty-two one-hot encoded sequences, each a hundred residues long with twenty numbers per residue, is thirty-two by one hundred by twenty.

64
00:05:44.475 --> 00:05:53.700
Now here is the practical advice, and I am going to repeat it in every session where you write code: always know the shape of every object you are handling.

65
00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.475
Most of the errors you will hit in this course are shape errors.

66
00:05:57.475 --> 00:06:02.725
Not conceptual mistakes: two rectangles of numbers that do not fit together.

67
00:06:02.725 --> 00:06:09.150
If you can say, for every variable, what its shape is, you will fix those in seconds instead of minutes.

68
00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:11.775
If you cannot, you will stare at them.

69
00:06:11.775 --> 00:06:14.550
Printing a shape costs one line.

70
00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.700
Get in the habit now.

71
00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:20.750
And here is the operation this whole block is for.

72
00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:26.525
The dot product of two vectors: multiply them entry by entry, and add up the results.

73
00:06:26.525 --> 00:06:30.525
First times first, plus second times second, and so on.

74
00:06:30.525 --> 00:06:32.700
One number comes out.

75
00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.600
Worked example, which we will use for the next three slides.

76
00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.425
Take the vector two, minus one and the vector one, four.

77
00:06:40.425 --> 00:06:42.725
Two times one is two.

78
00:06:42.725 --> 00:06:45.850
Minus one times four is minus four.

79
00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:48.850
Two plus minus four is minus two.

80
00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:51.650
So the dot product is minus two.

81
00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.675
Now, what does it mean?

82
00:06:53.675 --> 00:06:56.250
Read it as a weighted sum.

83
00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:00.050
One vector holds your data, the other holds weights.

84
00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:04.925
Each data value contributes to the total in proportion to its weight.

85
00:07:04.925 --> 00:07:15.725
A large positive weight on a feature means that feature pushes the total up; a negative weight means it pushes down; a weight near zero means that feature is essentially ignored.

86
00:07:15.725 --> 00:07:21.450
That reading, data times weights, added up, is what you will be doing for the rest of the course.

87
00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.850
A profile score is this.

88
00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:26.075
A perceptron is this.

89
00:07:26.075 --> 00:07:29.800
A layer of a neural network is a lot of these at once.

90
00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:38.825
The dot product also has a geometric meaning, and it is worth having both readings in your head because each is useful in different places.

91
00:07:38.825 --> 00:07:46.125
Geometrically, the dot product is the product of the two lengths, multiplied by the cosine of the angle between the vectors.

92
00:07:46.125 --> 00:07:51.525
You do not need that formula to use the important consequence, which is about the sign.

93
00:07:51.525 --> 00:08:01.000
If the two vectors point broadly in the same direction, the angle is less than ninety degrees, the cosine is positive, and the dot product is positive.

94
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:07.725
If they are perpendicular, the cosine of ninety degrees is zero, so the dot product is exactly zero.

95
00:08:07.725 --> 00:08:11.700
Perpendicular vectors have zero dot product, always.

96
00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:20.400
And if they point broadly in opposite directions, the angle is more than ninety degrees, the cosine is negative, and the dot product is negative.

97
00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.400
So the sign of a dot product tells you about agreement in direction, before you compute anything.

98
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:34.500
That is a useful instinct for later, when a dot product is a score: a positive score means the data points the way the weights do.

99
00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.950
Let us check that on our example.

100
00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:42.950
We found that the dot product of two, minus one and one, four is minus two.

101
00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:44.425
Negative.

102
00:08:44.425 --> 00:08:49.325
So the geometry says those two vectors must be more than ninety degrees apart.

103
00:08:49.325 --> 00:08:52.575
And they are: about a hundred and three degrees.

104
00:08:52.575 --> 00:08:54.975
Just past perpendicular.

105
00:08:54.975 --> 00:08:57.625
You can see that if you draw them.

106
00:08:57.625 --> 00:09:00.950
Two, minus one points right and slightly down.

107
00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:03.850
One, four points right and steeply up.

108
00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:10.350
They both have a rightward component, but the vertical components are opposite, and the vertical ones win.

109
00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:14.150
And the point of this slide is the direction of the inference.

110
00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:21.525
You did not need to compute an angle to know the sign, and you did not need to compute the dot product to know roughly what the angle was.

111
00:09:21.525 --> 00:09:23.925
Each tells you about the other.

112
00:09:23.925 --> 00:09:32.900
That is a small thing and it is how you develop a feel for whether a number you have computed is plausible, which is a skill this whole course cares about.

113
00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.450
And here is why I have spent four slides on one operation.

114
00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:42.750
In Session five you will build a profile, a position-specific scoring matrix.

115
00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:50.650
To score a candidate sequence you take each position, look up the score for the residue found there, and add all those scores together.

116
00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.900
That is a dot product.

117
00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:58.000
Data on one side, position-specific weights on the other, multiply and add.

118
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:03.525
In Session seven you will meet the perceptron, which is called the simplest neural network.

119
00:10:03.525 --> 00:10:10.725
It takes its inputs, multiplies each by a weight, adds them up, adds a bias, and passes the result through a function.

120
00:10:10.725 --> 00:10:13.350
That is the same dot product.

121
00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:20.275
So the oldest method in this course, from the nineteen eighties, and the foundation of the newest, compute the same thing.

122
00:10:20.275 --> 00:10:28.075
What differs is where the weights come from: a profile's weights are counted from an alignment, and a perceptron's are learned from examples.

123
00:10:28.075 --> 00:10:31.600
That distinction is the subject of Session seven.

124
00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:36.000
When you get there, almost nothing will be new except the interpretation.

125
00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:43.175
That is worth knowing in advance, because the vocabulary around neural networks makes them sound more exotic than they are.

126
00:10:43.775 --> 00:10:47.400
Now scale it up, which is all a matrix product is.

127
00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:53.375
Suppose you have three proteins and two features each, hydrophobic fraction and charged fraction.

128
00:10:53.375 --> 00:10:56.175
That is a three by two matrix.

129
00:10:56.175 --> 00:11:00.250
And you have a weight vector of two numbers, two and minus one.

130
00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:05.775
Multiply the matrix by the vector and you get three numbers: one score per protein.

131
00:11:05.775 --> 00:11:11.975
And each of those three numbers is exactly the dot product of that protein's row with the weight vector.

132
00:11:11.975 --> 00:11:17.425
So a matrix-vector product is one dot product per row, computed all at once.

133
00:11:17.425 --> 00:11:22.975
That is not a mathematical nicety; it is how all the code in this course is written.

134
00:11:22.975 --> 00:11:36.300
With a thousand proteins and twenty features, the data matrix is a thousand by twenty, the weight vector has twenty entries, and the product has a thousand entries: a thousand scores from one operation, with no loop.

135
00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:47.350
And that matters for speed, because that single operation runs in optimised compiled code, often on a graphics card, while a Python loop over a thousand proteins does not.

136
00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:54.050
When you see code in Session seven that scores an entire dataset in one line, this is what it is doing.

137
00:11:54.650 --> 00:12:00.675
The rule for whether two matrices can be multiplied, which is the rule you will break most often.

138
00:12:00.675 --> 00:12:06.725
If A has shape n by m and B has shape m by k, then A times B has shape n by k.

139
00:12:06.725 --> 00:12:12.200
The two inner numbers, the m and the m, must be equal, and they vanish from the result.

140
00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:26.100
Example from the slide: with three classes instead of one score, the weight matrix is twenty by three, so the data matrix, a thousand by twenty, times the weights, twenty by three, gives a thousand by three: three scores per protein.

141
00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:30.375
Now take a weight vector of length twenty-one instead of twenty.

142
00:12:30.375 --> 00:12:32.150
Nothing fits.

143
00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:36.875
Twenty and twenty-one do not match, and the operation is impossible.

144
00:12:36.875 --> 00:12:41.675
And this is, genuinely, the single most common error in this course's code.

145
00:12:41.675 --> 00:12:49.150
PyTorch will tell you something like: size mismatch, got input one thousand, mat one thousand by twenty, vec twenty-one.

146
00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:55.525
That message looks intimidating and it is actually complete: it has told you every shape involved.

147
00:12:55.525 --> 00:13:00.675
Read the numbers, find the pair that should be equal and is not, and you have the bug.

148
00:13:00.675 --> 00:13:03.050
That is the whole technique.

149
00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:10.400
The transpose: flip a matrix over its diagonal, so rows become columns and columns become rows.

150
00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:13.675
An n by m matrix becomes m by n.

151
00:13:13.675 --> 00:13:16.250
Three places you will meet it.

152
00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:25.900
Writing a dot product as a matrix product: w transpose times x is a one-by-m times an m-by-one, which gives a one-by-one, a single number.

153
00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:30.325
That is why you see dot products written with a transpose in papers.

154
00:13:30.325 --> 00:13:35.025
Second: X transpose times X, where X is your data matrix.

155
00:13:35.025 --> 00:13:42.800
If X is a thousand by twenty, then X transpose is twenty by a thousand, and the product is twenty by twenty.

156
00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:45.475
One entry per pair of features.

157
00:13:45.475 --> 00:13:57.175
For data that has been centred, that matrix divided by n minus one is the covariance matrix, which is the matrix that principal component analysis works on, two slides from now.

158
00:13:57.175 --> 00:14:04.175
Third, a rule worth knowing: the transpose of a product is the product of the transposes in the reverse order.

159
00:14:04.175 --> 00:14:08.200
A B transpose equals B transpose A transpose.

160
00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:12.500
And you can check that with shapes alone rather than memorising it.

161
00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:18.975
If A is n by m and B is m by k, then A B is n by k, so its transpose is k by n.

162
00:14:18.975 --> 00:14:22.725
Now, which order of the transposes gives k by n?

163
00:14:22.725 --> 00:14:29.700
B transpose is k by m, A transpose is m by n, so B transpose A transpose is k by n.

164
00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:32.450
The other order does not even fit.

165
00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:34.750
The shapes force the answer.

166
00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:43.700
Normalising a vector means dividing it by its own length, so that what is left has length exactly one, pointing in the same direction.

167
00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:50.725
Our example: the vector two, minus one has length the square root of five, which is about two point two three six.

168
00:14:50.725 --> 00:14:57.075
So the normalised version is about nought point eight nine four and minus nought point four four seven.

169
00:14:57.075 --> 00:15:00.200
Check it: square those, add, and you get one.

170
00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:01.850
Why do this?

171
00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:05.200
Because of the geometric formula from earlier.

172
00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:09.550
The dot product is length times length times the cosine of the angle.

173
00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:16.800
If both vectors have length one, the lengths drop out and the dot product is exactly the cosine of the angle between them.

174
00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:21.400
That number has a name you will see constantly: cosine similarity.

175
00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:32.650
It runs from minus one, for vectors pointing in opposite directions, through zero for perpendicular, to one for the same direction, and it measures direction only, ignoring magnitude.

176
00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:41.925
We used it in Session eleven to show that a leucine inside a signal peptide has a different representation from a leucine in a cytoplasmic loop.

177
00:15:41.925 --> 00:15:48.625
It is the standard way of comparing two vectors when you care about what they point at rather than how big they are.

178
00:15:48.625 --> 00:15:57.550
One footnote: the same length formula applied to every entry of a matrix gives the Frobenius norm, which is just the length of the matrix flattened out.

179
00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:01.450
And the first discussion problem is about exactly this.

180
00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:07.400
The last idea in this block, and the one I want you to take least algebra away from.

181
00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:14.300
When you multiply a vector by a matrix, you usually get a vector pointing somewhere else: the matrix turns it.

182
00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:21.850
But for some special vectors, the result points in exactly the same direction as the original, only longer or shorter.

183
00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:27.600
Those are the eigenvectors, and the factor by which each one is stretched is its eigenvalue.

184
00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:31.050
Written: A times v equals lambda times v.

185
00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:36.325
Here is the worked example, and notice I am going to check rather than solve.

186
00:16:36.325 --> 00:16:39.875
For the matrix on the slide, take the vector one, one.

187
00:16:39.875 --> 00:16:42.650
Multiply, and you get three, three.

188
00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:45.250
That is three times one, one.

189
00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:48.275
Same direction, three times as long.

190
00:16:48.275 --> 00:16:52.200
So one, one is an eigenvector with eigenvalue three.

191
00:16:52.200 --> 00:17:00.350
Now take one, minus one: multiplying gives back one, minus one, unchanged, so that is an eigenvector with eigenvalue one.

192
00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:07.950
And every other vector gets turned somewhat towards the one, one axis, because that is the direction being stretched most.

193
00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.950
I want to be clear about what you need here.

194
00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:14.775
You do not need to be able to find eigenvectors by hand.

195
00:17:14.775 --> 00:17:18.700
In this course the computer does it, with one function call.

196
00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:23.925
What you need is the idea: special directions that a matrix only stretches.

197
00:17:23.925 --> 00:17:27.925
Because that is what principal component analysis is.

198
00:17:27.925 --> 00:17:39.200
PCA finds the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, which are the directions in which your data varies most, and the eigenvalues say how much variance each direction accounts for.

199
00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:45.225
When somebody plots the first two principal components of a dataset, that is what they have done.

